<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tikar people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tikar are a people of the Tikar plain in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>. Many of the ruling dynasties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon-grassfields">Cameroon Grassfields</a>, including those of Bamum, Nso and Bafut, trace their origins to Tikar princes or princesses who left their homeland and founded new kingdoms, so that Tikar origin became a mark of royal descent. Their language belongs to the Bantoid branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>–Congo family.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tikar-people.jpg" alt="Tikar people" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Happiraphael · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FluteNordOuest.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tikar have also been known as Tikari, Tigar, Tigari and Tigre, and in the time of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-njoya">Njoya of Bamum</a> the missionary Henri Martin recorded that the Bamum took the name to mean those who wander. Some oral traditions trace them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, and some sources to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a>; researchers disagree whether any evidence supports this. The historian Eldridge Mohammadou proposed that the Tikar kingdoms were founded in the 18th century by Bare-Chamba invaders who overthrew Mbum rulers, but the anthropologist David Zeitlyn found that argument short of concrete evidence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mbum oral tradition tells of a Princess Wou-Ten, also called Betaka, who left the Kingdom of Nganha for the Adamawa Region and founded Tinkala, held to be the first Tikar fondom. In the late 14th century the brothers Tinki and Guié founded the kingdoms of Bankim, at Rifum, and Ngambé-Tikar. In one account their descendants then founded Bamum, Nso and Nditam, and later Ngoumé, Gâ and Kong; in another, Tinki's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nchare-yen">Nchare Yen</a> fled a succession struggle and founded Bamum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foumban">Foumban</a> in the late 14th or early 15th century, while his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngonnso">Ngonnso</a> founded Nso at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbo">Kumbo</a>.</p><p>Anthropologists and historians accept Tikar lineage claims only for the Kom, Nso, Bamum, Ndop-Bamunka and Bafut, and E. M. Chilver and Phyllis Mary Kaberry thought smaller groups' claims often political.</p><p>In the 18th and 19th centuries Chamba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> slavers, drawn by Tikar iron-working, preyed on the Tikar. Many captives from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> left from the Bight of Biafra, but numbers of Tikar and Duala were sold on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and shipped from there to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, where they formed a large share of the enslaved Cameroonians. The losses brought a steep decline in the Tikar population, though those who remained kept their heritage alive through oral tradition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Six adjoining Tikar kingdoms remain: Bankim, Ngambé-Tikar, Kong, Nditam, Ngoumé and Gâ, within boundaries unchanged since German colonisation. The Tikar language has four dialects, among them Túmú, spoken in Bankim and Nditam, and lacks the classical Bantu noun-class system. Most Tikar practise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> today, with smaller numbers following traditional religion or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. They call God the Creator Nyuy and revere their lineage ancestors, whose spirits were traditionally held to dwell in their preserved skulls. The Tikar are known for masks with strongly defined noses and large eyes, decorated brass pipes and carved horns and trumpets, and Toghu and Ndop cloth mark them and other Grassfields peoples.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tikar people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bafut was founded by groups said to have migrated from the <b>Tikar</b> area in the north-east, probably in the 18th century, and settled north of what is now Bamenda.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bafut">Kingdom of Bafut</a></li><li><blockquote>Bamum tradition traces the dynasty to Nchare Yen, a <b>Tikar</b> prince who conquered the area around Foumban and became its first Mfon, traditionally in 1394.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bamum">Kingdom of Bamum</a></li><li><blockquote>Nso tradition names Ngonnso, a <b>Tikar</b> princess who left her home with followers, as the founder of the kingdom, traditionally around the late 14th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-nso">Kingdom of Nso</a></li></ul></section>
